    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the help. so if it's a capital expenditure, does that mean i cant just deduct the whole thing this year? i'm also a bit worried about how to track small, random things i buy for the studio. sometimes it feels like little bits of money just disappear or get lost in my accounts and i can't account for them. how do i make sure i'm keeping everything organized so nothing slips through the cracks?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If it's a capital expenditure, you usually can't deduct the entire cost in the year you buy it; instead, you depreciate it over its useful life. For those small, random purchases, the key is to adopt a strict system, perhaps using a dedicated \"Office Supplies\" or \"Miscellaneous Expenses\" category in QuickBooks. Treat every single receipt like it's a valuable piece of evidence, and make it a habit to log *everything* immediately. This meticulous tracking is what keeps your books clean and prevents those little expenses from vanishing into the ether.",
      "source": "target",
