      "content": "To audit-proof those small expenses, you need consistency, not just random labels. Instead of labeling them vaguely, group them logically, like \"Design Software Subscriptions,\" \"Office Consumables,\" or \"Client Meeting Refreshments.\" Even if the amounts are tiny, having a clear, consistent category for each type of purchase shows an auditor that you have a systematic approach to your spending. Don't just dump them into a single \"Misc.\" bucket; that's where the suspicion starts to creep in.",
